#### Phase 1: Rule Engine Setup

1. **Select Rule Engine**:
   - **Option A - RulesEngine** (Microsoft, MIT license, JSON-based)
   - **Option B - CoreRulesEngine** (.NET Core, lightweight)
   - **Option C - NRules** (forward-chaining, Rete algorithm)
   - **Option D - Custom** (if specific DSL needed)

2. **Install RulesEngine NuGet Package**:
   ```bash
   dotnet add package RulesEngine
   ```

3. **Create Rule Configuration Storage**:
   ```csharp
   public interface IRuleRepository
   {
       Task<List<Rule>> GetRulesAsync(string workflowName, string tenantId);
       Task SaveRulesAsync(string workflowName, List<Rule> rules, string tenantId);
       Task<RuleVersion> GetRuleVersionAsync(string workflowName, string version);
   }

   public class JsonFileRuleRepository : IRuleRepository
   {
       private readonly string _rulesPath;

       public async Task<List<Rule>> GetRulesAsync(string workflowName, string tenantId)
       {
           var filePath = Path.Combine(_rulesPath, tenantId, $"{workflowName}.json");
           var json = await File.ReadAllTextAsync(filePath);
           var workflow = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Workflow>(json);
           return workflow.Rules;
       }
   }
   ```

4. **Initialize Rules Engine**:
   ```csharp
   public class TexasClaimValidationExtension : IClaimValidator
   {
       private readonly IRuleRepository _ruleRepository;
       private RulesEngine _rulesEngine;

       public async Task InitializeAsync(string tenantId)
       {
           var rules = await _ruleRepository.GetRulesAsync("TexasClaimValidation", tenantId);
           var workflow = new Workflow
           {
               WorkflowName = "TexasClaimValidation",
               Rules = rules
           };
           _rulesEngine = new RulesEngine(new[] { workflow }, null);
       }
   }
   ```

